{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'closure|f15423c2|BLOCK|environment|token|carrier|Completed' collab/exception-track/1794557000000-engineer-port-phase5-carrier.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log | tail -55; rg -l 'owner.*NO|Owner.*NO|:retained.*NO|NO.*:retained|amendment 5' collab/exception-track --glob '*.md'; cat .git; ls .git/refs/heads\"","aggregated_output":"3:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the architecture and review findings first, then trace the current exception lowering and add the carrier and host-side coverage within the stated boundaries.\"}}\n5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'compile-guest-call|unwind-to|call-ec|raise-exit|protocol' src/cljc/yang/clojure* test/yang/clojure_test.clj test/yang/core/call_test.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1412:  ;; `missing-raise-exit` surface. A dynamic call and a static intra-unit\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1457:;; The exception track, Clojure round R3: protocol failures retain and\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1475:(deftest test-exception-r3-protocol-condition-forwarded-unchanged\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1476:  (testing \\\"a protocol failure delivered to the guest's own raise continuation\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1650:  (testing \\\"P1-2: (:yang.call/reason e) on a caught protocol condition\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1662:          (str lane \\\": the protocol reason is visible on the caught value\\\"))))\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1757:  (testing \\\"3: an ordinary :dsg__condition field in a transferred protocol\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1834:  (testing \\\"(A) protocol: the retained wrapper answers its condition's\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1866:  ;; there is no retention object: a caught protocol condition is the plain\\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:1870:  (testing \\\"(A) a caught protocol condition is ordinary data, not a carrier\\\"\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:898:   escapes section, minus `call-ec`/`unwind-to` (clojure has no\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1382:(defn- compile-guest-call\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1386:   payload), any other value (a qualified protocol condition or an unmarked\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1396:          raise-exit (u/lam [condition]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1408:                             (u/lit []) raise-exit)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1424:                            raise-exit)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1457:    (compile-guest-call (compile-form f false env macro-env)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1553:    (compile-guest-call (compile-form operator false env macro-env)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:30:     `clj/raise`, `clj/call-ec`, `clj/unwind-to` and their helpers: the\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:135:    [clj/unwind-to\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:145:                 (clj/unwind-to n))))))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:165:    [clj/call-ec\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:173:                       (do (clj/unwind-to (clj/frame-depth (get saved :handlers)))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '850,980p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1360,1440p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; rg -n 'clj/raise|compile-try|handler|witness' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\";; under namespaced keys, `ex-message`/`ex-data` its accessors. Generated names\\n;; carry the reserved `dsg__` prefix; the program pre-pass keeps user code out\\n;; of that prefix. A macro call is opaque to the pass: the expander owns it.\\n\\n(def ^:private ok-tag :yin.k/ok)\\n\\n(def ^:private error-tag :yin.k/error)\\n\\n\\n(defn- outcomes?\\n  [env]\\n  (some? (::outcomes env)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- binder-symbols\\n  [b]\\n  (into #{} (comp (filter symbol?) (remove #{'&})) (tree-seq coll? seq b)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- rebuild\\n  \\\"A list of `items` carrying the reader metadata of `form`.\\\"\\n  [form items]\\n  (let [out (apply list items)]\\n    (if-let [m (meta form)]\\n      (with-meta out m)\\n      out)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The clj continuation family (R3 §8.10.7; the (B) port)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;;\\n;; The port converges clojure's exception lowering onto the continuation\\n;; encoding: a `%capture` handler stack in a dynamic-context cell, exactly\\n;; like the py/ts/php preludes (`yang.clojure.prelude` carries the reference\\n;; implementation under `clj/`). A source `(throw e)` lowers to `clj/throw`,\\n;; `(try ...)` to `clj/try`/`clj/try-finally`, and function bodies return\\n;; plain values. A guest call delivers a callee's escaping condition through\\n;; the §8.10.7 raise exit, and the public entry installs the same bridge.\\n;; The caught value IS the payload/condition — no wrapper, no retention\\n;; object, no per-call-site routing. `compile-program` emits this family\\n;; inline (the linked runtime binds the same family from the prelude); it\\n;; routes the capture-sensitive primitives through the `dsg__*` helpers so a\\n;; program definition of `get`/`assoc`/`=` cannot capture a frame access.\\n\\n(def ^:private continuation-family\\n  \\\"The `clj/` continuation family, `[key form]` in prelude notation (each\\n   form is read with `u/sexp->uast`). Mirrors `yang.clojure.prelude`'s\\n   escapes section, minus `call-ec`/`unwind-to` (clojure has no\\n   break/continue/return escapes). The handler stack is a chain of frames\\n   [tag payload rest n]; a `:handler` frame holds the continuation\\n   `clj/raise` delivers to, a `:finally` frame a one-argument thunk every\\n   exit through it runs.\\\"\\n  '[[clj/ctx (fn [hs] (dsg__assoc {} :handlers hs))]\\n    [clj/handlers (fn [] (dsg__get (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers))]\\n    [clj/set-handlers!\\n     (fn [hs]\\n       (cell/set! clj.rt/ctx (dsg__assoc (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers hs)))]\\n    [clj/restore! (fn [saved] (cell/set! clj.rt/ctx saved))]\\n    [clj/frame-depth (fn [hs] (if (dsg__equal hs nil) 0 (dsg__get hs 3)))]\\n    [clj/frame\\n     (fn [tag payload rest]\\n       (conj (conj (conj (conj [] tag) payload) rest)\\n             (+ 1 (clj/frame-depth rest))))]\\n    [clj/pop-frame\\n     (fn [hs]\\n       (cell/set! clj.rt/ctx\\n                  (dsg__assoc (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers (dsg__get hs 2))))]\\n    [clj/raise\\n     (fn [e]\\n       (let [hs (clj/handlers)]\\n         (if (dsg__equal hs nil)\\n           (dsg__get e :clj/no-handler)\\n           (if (dsg__equal (dsg__get hs 0) :finally)\\n             (do (clj/pop-frame hs) ((dsg__get hs 1) e) (clj/raise e))\\n             ((dsg__get hs 1) e)))))]\\n    [clj/throw (fn [e] (clj/raise e))]\\n    [clj/try\\n     (fn [body handler]\\n       (let [saved (cell/get clj.rt/ctx)\\n             flag (cell/new :first)]\\n         ((fn [r]\\n            (if (dsg__equal (cell/get flag) :first)\\n              (do (cell/set! flag :re-entered)\\n                  (clj/set-handlers! (clj/frame :handler r (dsg__get saved :handlers)))\\n                  (let [v (body)] (do (clj/restore! saved) v)))\\n              (do (clj/restore! saved) (handler r))))\\n          (%capture))))]\\n    [clj/try-finally\\n     (fn [body fin]\\n       (let [saved (cell/get clj.rt/ctx)]\\n         (do (clj/set-handlers! (clj/frame :finally fin (dsg__get saved :handlers)))\\n             (let [v (body)]\\n               (do (clj/restore! saved) (fin nil) v)))))]])\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private ex-message-form\\n  '(fn [dsg__value] (dsg__get dsg__value :yin.k/message)))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private ex-data-form\\n  '(fn [dsg__value] (dsg__get dsg__value :yin.k/data)))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private continuation-uast\\n  \\\"The continuation family tree: define every `clj/` entry (store-defined,\\n   like `yang.core.call/uast`) then allocate the `clj.rt/ctx` cell. The\\n   family's capture-sensitive primitives are the `dsg__*` names, bound at\\n   emission by `compile-program`.\\\"\\n  (u/then\\n    (reduce (fn [ast [key form]]\\n              (u/then ast (u/def! key (u/sexp->uast form))))\\n            (u/lit nil)\\n            continuation-family)\\n    (u/def! 'clj.rt/ctx\\n            (u/app (u/v 'cell/new) (u/app (u/v 'clj/ctx) (u/lit nil))))))\\n\\n\\n(def continuation-family-names\\n  \\\"The `clj/` continuation-family store definitions `compile-program` emits\\n   inline (plus the `clj.rt/ctx` cell): compiler-bound names an admission scan\\n   must accept, like the preamble's `dsg__*` helpers. Exposed so a harness\\n   admission check stays in sync with the family instead of hardcoding it.\\\"\\n  (into #{'clj.rt/ctx} (map first continuation-family)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- rewrite-ex-info\\n  \\\"(ex-info msg data cause?) as the portable map holding its parts. The\\n   accessors read through the generated exception wrapper: a retained\\n   condition's wrapper carries the original payload privately, so\\n   (ex-message e)/(ex-data e) answer the payload's parts while a plain\\n  (let [seal (u/app (u/v 'dsg__get) callee (u/lit :seal))]\\n    (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'dsg__equal)\\n                      (u/app (u/v 'dsg__get) callee (u/lit :type))\\n                      (u/lit :cell-ref))\\n               (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'data/contains?) callee (u/lit :id))\\n                          (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'data/contains?) callee (u/lit :seal))\\n                                     (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'dsg__equal)\\n                                                       (u/app (u/v 'data/count) callee)\\n                                                       (u/lit 3))\\n                                                (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'data/string?) seal)\\n                                                           (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'dsg__equal)\\n                                                                             (u/app (u/v 'data/str-length) seal)\\n                                                                             (u/lit 64))\\n                                                                      (u/lit true)\\n                                                                      (u/lit false))\\n                                                           (u/lit false))\\n                                                (u/lit false))\\n                                     (u/lit false))\\n                          (u/lit false))\\n               (u/lit false))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- compile-guest-call\\n  \\\"Admit a source operator before common invocation. The raise exit delivers\\n   the callee's escaping condition into the clj continuation: a\\n   `:yang.call/thrown` condition delivers its payload (the caught value IS the\\n   payload), any other value (a qualified protocol condition or an unmarked\\n   value) delivers whole. Only generated UAST implementation calls apply\\n   private closures directly.\\\"\\n  [callee-ast arguments-ast env tail?]\\n  (if-not (outcomes? env)\\n    (cond-> (u/app callee-ast arguments-ast)\\n      tail? (assoc :tail? true))\\n    (let [callee (symbol (str reserved-prefix \\\"callee\\\"))\\n          arguments (symbol (str reserved-prefix \\\"arguments\\\"))\\n          condition (symbol (str reserved-prefix \\\"condition\\\"))\\n          raise-exit (u/lam [condition]\\n                            (assoc (u/app (u/v 'clj/raise)\\n                                          (u/if-node\\n                                            (u/app (u/v 'dsg__equal)\\n                                                   (u/app (u/v 'dsg__get) (u/v condition)\\n                                                          (u/lit :yang.call/reason))\\n                                                   (u/lit :yang.call/thrown))\\n                                            (u/app (u/v 'dsg__get) (u/v condition)\\n                                                   (u/lit :yang.call/value))\\n                                            (u/v condition)))\\n                                   :tail? true))\\n          common-call (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/invoke) (u/v callee) (u/v arguments)\\n                             (u/lit []) raise-exit)\\n          refused (u/app (u/v 'clj/raise)\\n                         (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/condition)\\n                                (u/lit :yang.call/not-callable)\\n                                (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/lit {})\\n                                       (u/lit :yang.call/callee) (u/v callee))))\\n          admitted (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v callee))\\n                              common-call refused)\\n          ;; A callee carrying a call entry is convention territory whether it\\n          ;; admits or forges one, so admission rules it. Everything else is a\\n          ;; data-side invocation - a collection lookup or a raw closure call -\\n          ;; owned by the guest library's invoker, reached the same way.\\n          value-call (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/invoke)\\n                            (u/v 'invoke-value)\\n                            (u/app (u/v 'conj) (u/lit []) (u/v callee) (u/v arguments))\\n                            (u/lit [])\\n                            raise-exit)\\n          routed (if (::invoker env)\\n                   (u/if-node\\n                     (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'data/map?) (u/v callee))\\n                                (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'data/contains?) (u/v callee) (u/lit :yang.call/entry))\\n                                           (u/lit true)\\n                                           ;; the shape is only WHEN to ask (ruling of\\n                                           ;; record, rule 2): the heap answers\\n                                           ;; `cell/authentic?`, and an unauthenticated\\n                                           ;; shape takes ordinary map invocation\\n                                           (u/if-node (cell-ref-shaped-node (u/v callee))\\n                                                      (u/app (u/v 'cell/authentic?) (u/v callee))\\n                                                      (u/lit false)))\\n                                (u/lit false))\\n                     admitted\\n                     value-call)\\n                   admitted)]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:845:;; to `clj/try`/`clj/try-finally` (the `%capture` handler-stack family), and a\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:883:;; encoding: a `%capture` handler stack in a dynamic-context cell, exactly\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:899:   break/continue/return escapes). The handler stack is a chain of frames\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:900:   [tag payload rest n]; a `:handler` frame holds the continuation\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:901:   `clj/raise` delivers to, a `:finally` frame a one-argument thunk every\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:903:  '[[clj/ctx (fn [hs] (dsg__assoc {} :handlers hs))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904:    [clj/handlers (fn [] (dsg__get (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:905:    [clj/set-handlers!\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:907:       (cell/set! clj.rt/ctx (dsg__assoc (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers hs)))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:917:                  (dsg__assoc (cell/get clj.rt/ctx) :handlers (dsg__get hs 2))))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:918:    [clj/raise\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:920:       (let [hs (clj/handlers)]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:922:           (dsg__get e :clj/no-handler)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:924:             (do (clj/pop-frame hs) ((dsg__get hs 1) e) (clj/raise e))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:926:    [clj/throw (fn [e] (clj/raise e))]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:928:     (fn [body handler]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:934:                  (clj/set-handlers! (clj/frame :handler r (dsg__get saved :handlers)))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:936:              (do (clj/restore! saved) (handler r))))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:941:         (do (clj/set-handlers! (clj/frame :finally fin (dsg__get saved :handlers)))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1055: