{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794456002000-to-yangclojure-port-adopts-provenance-primitive.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794450000000-crosslang-handoff-vm-provenance.md | sed -n '1,170p'; nl -ba test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj | sed -n '1,90p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 01:30:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 08:30:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / deepseek-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tOwner decision requested: ONE question. Nothing merges until you answer or until the shiva\n     5\tcontract numbers independently prove the same thing.\n     6\t\n     7\t# OWNER DECISION — may yang.clojure's fix add a VM-level retained-cell mark?\n     8\t\n     9\t## How we got here (one paragraph)\n    10\t\n    11\tClojure R3 (protocol-condition retention through the public escape) took four engineer rounds and\n    12\tthree independent confirmations. Each round's tests were green; each reviewer found a real defect\n    13\tthe tests could not see: a token transplant (R5), then a payload-identity regression that turned a\n    14\tRound-0 green cross-language row red, then content replay/mutation plus seven observation leaks.\n    15\tA three-architect mob was convened on whether a wrapper can ever be observationally identical to\n    16\tits payload: **2/3 ruled (A)** — deliver ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads RAW (so the caught\n    17\tvalue IS the payload and every leak closes by construction), and keep the wrapper ONLY for\n    18\tqualified protocol conditions, bound to heap identity. muse-wonder (the dissenting (B) author)\n    19\timplemented (A) and returned **\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\"**; glm-5.3 (author of the (A) rationale)\n    20\tconfirmed it independently: **CONFIRMED**.\n    21\t\n    22\t## What the fix does now (`fbd8da0f`, branch `exception-r3-impl`)\n    23\t\n    24\t1. `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` — ordinary payloads raw; wrapper for protocol conditions only;\n    25\t   the per-call-site router and the witness closure DELETED. This half alone closes all seven of\n    26\t   the reviewer's observation leaks and restores the Round-0 round-trip row.\n    27\t2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` + `module.cljc` (+82 lines) — an issuer-side `:retained` mark on a\n    28\t   heap entry, written only by a compiler-internal `:cell/new-retained` effect published as\n    29\t   `cell/dsg__new-retained` (a name guest source cannot spell); `:cell/set!` refuses marked cells;\n    30\t   a pure `cell/retained?` query beside `cell/authentic?`.\n    31\t\n    32\t## The decision\n    33\t\n    34\tglm's adjudication, verbatim in substance: *the seam is SAFE (521 tests / 6,188 assertions green\n    35\tacross the VM seam and all lanes; no invariant breakage), but AUTHORIZATION NEEDS-OWNER — the\n    36\tmob's (A) included this seam, while the track's written authorization named only the (B) fallback.*\n    37\t\n    38\t**Question for the owner: may the cell module publish the issuer-side retained mint/query, and may\n    39\t`:cell/set!` refuse marked cells — i.e. may R3 touch yin.vm at all?**\n    40\t\n    41\t- **YES** → clojure R3 merges as `fbd8da0f` (pending the shiva contract numbers), retention is\n    42\t  identity-bound and unforgeable by construction, and the `:retained` mark becomes a documented VM\n    43\t  seam with a `state.md` heap-shape correction.\n    44\t- **NO** → the raw-payload half still lands (it is separable: revert the two wrapper forms to the\n    45\t  witness versions). Consequence: protocol-condition retention keeps the witness-but-not-identity\n    46\t  binding, so the content-replay/mutation finding from round 3 stays OPEN and must either be\n    47\t  accepted as a known limitation (with the `:forged-retention` row documenting it) or deferred to\n    48\t  a later VM round.\n    49\t- **DEFER** → I hold `fbd8da0f` unmerged, land the raw-payload half alone, and the VM seam becomes\n    50\t  a separate proposal.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Evidence trail\n    53\t\n    54\t- Mob record: `1794369000000-mob-record-r3-retention-design.md` (2/3 (A), 1/3 (B), votes converge)\n    55\t- Design: `1794366000000-architect-mob-r3-retention-design.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    56\t- Implementation verdict: `1794370000000-engineer-r3-glmsplit-implementation.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log`\n    57\t  (\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\", 103 tests/993 assertions)\n    58\t- Independent confirmation: `1794371000000-reviewer-r3-A-design-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    59\t  (CONFIRMED; seam SAFE; authorization NEEDS-OWNER; separable)\n    60\t- Foundational finding: `1794365000000-reviewer-r3-identity-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log`\n    61\t\n    62\t---\n    63\t\n    64\t## DECIDED: **NO** (owner, 2026-11-11 16:3x +07, in-session — verbatim: \"NO\")\n    65\t\n    66\tyin.vm stays untouched. The `:retained` heap mark, `cell/dsg__new-retained` mint, `cell/retained?`\n    67\tquery, and the `:cell/set!` refusal on marked entries are NOT authorized.\n    68\t\n    69\t### Consequences recorded\n    70\t\n    71\t1. R3 lands as the **raw-payload half only**: ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads delivered raw\n    72\t   (closes all seven observation leaks; restores the round-trip row), protocol conditions retained\n    73\t   by the **witness mechanism** (c9c5533c's complete-contents closure), NOT heap identity.\n    74\t2. **Known limitation, documented here and in the ledger**: a guest that catches a protocol\n    75\t   wrapper can mutate its contents via `cell/set!` and substitute a different condition; the\n    76\t   boundary then forwards the substituted condition. Python/PHP do not have this hole (their\n    77\t   `ForeignException` is a native object with private state). This is a deliberate, owner-accepted\n    78\t   trade to keep yin.vm minimal.\n    79\t3. **R4's equivalence proof gains the decisive scenario**: does the outcome channel deliver\n    80\t   protocol-condition retention as securely as the continuation lane? The replay/mutation probes\n    81\t   (astra's battery) join the §8.10.9 scenario set. **Failure there triggers the owner's\n    82\t   pre-authorized (B) fallback** — under which the protocol carrier is expected to be a\n    83\t   closure-based immutable form (no VM change needed either).\n    84\t4. The rationale, owner's words in substance: adding VM lines to compensate for one frontend's\n    85\t   encoding inconsistency cuts against yin.vm's minimal-kernel design; the inconsistency is\n    86\t   handled at the layer that created it.\n    87\t\n    88\t---\n    89\t\n    90\t## FINAL DIRECTIVE (owner, 2026-11-11 17:0x +07, in-session) — SUPERSEDES the earlier reading of \"NO\"\n    91\t\n    92\tOwner, verbatim in substance across three messages: the \"NO\" vetoed **the divergence itself** —\n    93\t\"yang.clojure exception should converge. it should not be the exception.\" The VM seam was declined\n    94\tbecause kernel lines compensating for an encoding the owner does not want felt wrong — not because\n    95\tthe outcome encoding should be kept.\n    96\t\n    97\t**Decision of record: (B) TRIGGERS NOW.** yang.clojure's exception lowering converges onto the\n    98\tcontinuation encoding (the py/ts/php `%capture` raise-exit scheme). No VM change. R4 transforms\n    99\tfrom an equivalence proof into **port verification**; the earlier \"(A) now, (B) if R4 proof fails\"\n   100\tsequencing is superseded by this explicit directive.\n   101\t\n   102\t### The decisive argument (accepted by the owner through this exchange)\n   103\t\n   104\tReal Clojure — JVM, cljs, dart, jolt — uses dynamic unwinding with non-cooperating frames\n   105\t(throw-through-HOF works, `finally` runs from arbitrary depth), exactly the python/ts/php\n   106\tsemantics class; `recur` exists because those hosts lack TCO. yang.clojure's outcome model was a\n   107\tdivergence from **real Clojure itself**, forcing the restricted dialect (one catch, no finally,\n   108\topt-in outcomes). The port moves yang.clojure toward real Clojure semantics while yin.vm's native\n   109\tTCO + loop/recur (already compiling to true tail calls) preserves constant-depth iteration under\n   110\tany encoding.\n   111\t\n   112\t### Binding acceptance criteria (from the 2/2 depth mod, 1794396000000)\n   113\t\n   114\t1. LaTTe depth harness built FIRST; pre-port baselines recorded (the harness collects no depth today).\n   115\t2. The intra-unit private tail lane is PRESERVED in the port design (deepseek's remedy — no VM change).\n   116\t3. Uniform four-frontend probe: assert constant case only (`D(16)=D(64)=D(256)`, loop/recur +\n   117\t   handler-free chains); MEASURE the linear case at 8/32/128 × three categories; never require\n   118\t   growth contractually.\n   119\t4. Escalation thresholds to the owner BEFORE merge: any LaTTe workload >10× pre-port max depth or\n   120\t   >10,000 frames post-port.\n   121\t5. PHP's missing R1 bridge noted — \"every published entry\" becomes true for all four frontends\n   122\t   when the php round lands; the uniformity this directive asks for is then complete.\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 14:07:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-12 03:07:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tTo: yang.clojure orchestrator seat\n     5\t\n     6\t# One amendment to the port context (your cc3 sequence stands; consult amendment 5)\n     7\t\n     8\tSol's design consult (findings: `1794451000000-consult-vm-provenance-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`)\n     9\truled the cross-frontend adoption map for the provenance primitive, and it touches the port you\n    10\tare sequencing around:\n    11\t\n    12\t- **The port adopts the primitive for protocol-condition retention** (sol amendment 5). Sol\n    13\t  verified the port draft's \"no retention object needed\" claim FAILS the R3 protocol-rethrow\n    14\t  requirement: a clojure-native qualified condition caught and rethrown by clojure source needs\n    15\t  retention under the continuation encoding. The primitive replaces the witness mechanism — and\n    16\t  retires the replay/mutation limitation documented under your earlier NO.\n    17\t- Your landing sequence is otherwise untouched: **cc3 proceeds, merge first**; the port rebases\n    18\t  onto post-cc3 master and takes the latte merge; your post-port oracle-binding re-verification\n    19\t  obligation stands (the preserved intra-unit private tail lane is the shape your binding\n    20\t  targets — unchanged by the primitive, which lives in heap entries, not the tail lane).\n    21\t- The VM round implementing the primitive is in flight (`exception-vm-provenance`, deepseek-v4-pro).\n    22\t  The port's protocol-retention phase dispatches after both the primitive lands and cc3 merges.\n    23\t\n    24\tNo action for your seat beyond what your ack already commits. — the steward\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 1x:xx GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 1x:xx +0700\n     3\tFrom: yang.python orchestrator seat (round owner: exception-py-r2r3)\n     4\tTo: a CROSS-LANGUAGE orchestrator taking responsibility for the VM provenance-storage work\n     5\tStatus: OWNER-AUTHORIZED (2026-11-11, \"yes\" + two conditions: engineer/architect consult; cross-frontend awareness ruling). You own what follows.\n     6\t\n     7\t# HANDOFF — the VM provenance-storage work: a cross-language implementation brief\n     8\t\n     9\tYou are taking over a piece of work that exceeds any single frontend seat: a VM-level change that four frontends will depend on, plus the design ruling that keeps them from diverging. This brief is self-contained. Read the three authority documents it names, verify nothing, trust the file:line evidence — it was all gathered in-tree today.\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. The defect (proven, not hypothesized)\n    12\t\n    13\tThe exception track's step-7 review (`collab/exception-track/1794402000000-architect-py-r2r3-step7-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log`, verdict FIX-LIST) confirmed by EXECUTED PROBE on all three JVM vector backends:\n    14\t\n    15\t- Python exception wrappers stamp their retained condition (the `{:yang.call/reason ... :yang.call/value ...}` record that must cross guest-call boundaries intact, ruling v2 §R2/R3) into **the guest-visible cell map** (`:py/retained-condition` key).\n    16\t- Any admitted guest program can forge that stamp. Executed: Clojure source `(defn forge [e condition] (cell/new (assoc (cell/get e) :py/retained-condition condition)))`; Python created `Exception('fresh')`, passed it through `forge`, raised the clone. Egress (python prelude.cljc:917) forwarded the attacker's condition as if runtime-minted (`:forged-condition-forwarded true`). The same cell ops overwrite a GENUINE wrapper's stamp.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 2. Why the fix is a VM change (the heap anatomy — verified in-tree today)\n    19\t\n    20\t- `cell/new` allocates a heap entry (yin.vm/engine.cljc:2773): fresh id, sealed `:cell-ref`, `heap-write` of the content.\n    21\t- The guest-visible map is **the entry's `:value` field**: `cell/get` answers only `[:heap id :value]` (:2780, \"the content is answered as data, never re-interpreted\"); `cell/set!` writes only `:value` (:2784). The guest has NO operation addressing any other field of the entry.\n    22\t- Therefore: the stamp must move OUT of `:value` into a **host-only sibling field on the entry** (e.g. `:yang.call/provenance`, sibling of `:seal`/`:value`) — write-once when the runtime mints a wrapper, read only by the host's identity check, never guest-visible, reclaimed by the existing GC with the entry (it lives in the same heap map; no registry, no leak).\n    23\t- `cell/authentic?` alone is insufficient — the forged clone is a genuinely allocated cell with a genuine seal. The lie is in the `:value` content, not the cell's identity.\n    24\t- The JS seat fixed the SAME P1 differently: inside `js.rt/*` (commits `31287b17` \"private identity-store provenance — membership, not wrapper metadata\" + `1697b257`), because JS wrappers are js-runtime objects — their store lives in their own surface, one layer ABOVE the VM heap. Python wrappers are VM cells; python cannot replicate the js.rt approach in-surface. PHP wrappers will presumably also be VM cells → PHP inherits this problem the moment its R1+R2+R3 round starts.\n    25\t\n    26\t## 3. The owner's decision (verbatim conditions)\n    27\t\n    28\tAuthorized (\"yes\"), with: (a) **consult with an engineer or architect** on the design before landing; (b) **rule on cross-frontend awareness** — do the other yang.* compilers need to apply this in their pipelines?\n    29\t\n    30\t## 4. What YOU now own (the cross-language responsibility)\n    31\t\n    32\t1. **The consult**: run the engineer/architect design review of the storage design — host-only entry field, write-once at wrapper mint, host-identity read, GC-with-entry, no guest-visible vocabulary unless unavoidable (if unavoidable: full `cell/authentic?` rigor — declared API change, second opinion). Route per routing-status.md; sol carried the deep review rounds well this arc.\n    33\t2. **The cross-frontend ruling** (the owner's condition (b)): recommend a SHARED primitive — one VM provenance mechanism all wrapper-minting frontends adopt — rather than three divergent schemes. The known consumers: python (now), php (at its round's start), clojure (open question: its outcome channel is internal per decision (A); whether its boundary retained wrappers need VM provenance is part of the ruling), js (already self-sufficient via js.rt — decide whether to migrate it onto the shared primitive or grandfather it; migration is NOT urgent, js's fix is sound).\n    34\t3. **The surface sequencing**: the change touches `yin.vm.engine`/`yin.vm.module` (+ python init/attachment; + php/js/clojure adoption as ruled). The yin.vm seat owns the VM surface — notify them via the surface registry's queue rule (`1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md`) and check contention before any round writes there. Designate the writing seat per round (a yin.vm-seat round for the engine field, then per-frontend adoption rounds), one writer per file+region as always.\n    35\t4. **The acceptance instrument** (already written, currently RED on all three backends — do not rewrite it, it is the contract): the three forgery pins in `test/yang/call/python_exception_test.cljc:108` — the reviewer's exact forge pair (after the fix: a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` whose payload is the clone, never the forged condition), a cell-clone-of-genuine pin, and an overwrite-attempt pin. No-regression gate: the eight python ledger fixtures + the parity pins (`be87df74`) + the shared suites.\n    36\t5. **The ledger**: append the round delta per the ledger's append-by-update rule when accepted; the py round (`exception-py-r2r3`, tip `16c6294f` + goldens `04cb7887`) is otherwise ACCEPTED and merge-held only on this fix.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 5. The constraints that bind you (so the brief is complete)\n    39\t\n    40\t- Ruling v2 (`1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md`) §R2's lifetime rules: identity-owned, outside source-writable attributes, no ambient registry, wrapper non-stacking, formatting never invokes foreign methods.\n    41\t- Decision (A) (`1794341000000`): no internal-encoding convergence — the storage primitive is shared INFRASTRUCTURE, not encoding convergence.\n    42\t- R5: nothing may route on condition-map shape; the provenance check is identity, not shape.\n    43\t- The `^:slow` gates stay; the contract test is never run locally on the laptop (owner ruling); remote acceptance runs carry the overlay-inspection rule (post-checkout `git diff --stat` must equal the intended overlay — two contamination incidents this arc).\n    44\t- The surface registry's one-writer rule and the branch registry (`exception-py-r2r3` is the python seat's; the VM round needs its own branch and its own owner — designate it).\n    45\t\n    46\t## 6. State of record when you take over\n    47\t\n    48\t- py branch `exception-py-r2r3` @ `16c6294f` (+ `04cb7887`): ACCEPTED in the ledger (24 rows green, xtrack-py2 no-overlay), merge-held on this fix.\n    49\t- js branch `exception-js-r1r3` @ `40284c15`: js5 re-acceptance in flight.\n    50\t- clojure branch `exception-r3-impl` @ `fbd8da0f`: accepted (18 rows green, xtrack-r3A4); clojure now additionally carries the owner-directed continuation-port work.\n    51\t- php round: not started; its gate was js5-green.\n    52\t- The fix round's artifacts: pins in the worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12` (branch `exception-py-r2r3`, uncommitted — the round stopped pre-code awaiting your authorization chain; commit them as the VM round's G0 or fold them into the VM round's brief, your call as the sequencing owner).\n    53\t- My seat hands off ROUND OWNERSHIP of the cross-language fix but remains the python ADOPTION owner: once the primitive lands, the python round wires its wrapper minting/reading onto it and re-runs the same gates.\n    54\t\n    55\t— yang.python orchestrator seat\n     1\t(ns yang.call.exception-contract\n     2\t  \"Round 0's executable §8.10.7 provenance contract. Source strings are\n     3\t   parsed by the JVM kit, never patched after lowering. Boundary probes\n     4\t   alone may manufacture invocations that valid source cannot express.\n     5\t   `fixtures` is the acceptance-ledger input; pending is not a passing test.\n     6\t   The ruling, rather than today's runtime output, determines `:expected`.\"\n     7\t  (:require [clojure.string :as str]))\n     8\t\n     9\t\n    10\t(def frontends #{:clojure :typescript :php :python})\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(def protocol-reasons\n    14\t  [:yang.call/not-callable :yang.call/invalid-invocation\n    15\t   :yang.call/unsupported-binding])\n    16\t\n    17\t\n    18\t(def sources\n    19\t  \"Every export below is source-produced. `original` is an identity-bearing\n    20\t   value, including Clojure's explicit cell. `receive` catches then rethrows unchanged;\n    21\t   `retain` returns normally before `later` raises from a new activation.\n    22\t   Protocol catch forms are intentionally local catch-all forms. No guessed\n    23\t   donor class name participates in reception.\"\n    24\t  {:python\n    25\t   (str/join \"\\n\"\n    26\t             [\"original = ValueError('original')\"\n    27\t              \"saved = None\"\n    28\t              \"def payload():\" \"    return original\"\n    29\t              \"def donor_raise():\" \"    raise original\"\n    30\t              \"def make_exploder():\"\n    31\t              \"    def inner():\" \"        raise original\" \"    return inner\"\n    32\t              \"def needs_argument(x):\" \"    return x\"\n    33\t              \"def receive(f):\"\n    34\t              \"    try:\" \"        return f()\"\n    35\t              \"    except BaseException as e:\" \"        raise\"\n    36\t              \"def retain(f):\" \"    global saved\"\n    37\t              \"    try:\" \"        f()\"\n    38\t              \"    except BaseException as e:\" \"        saved = e\" \"        return e\"\n    39\t              \"def later():\" \"    raise saved\"\n    40\t              \"def is_foreign(e):\"\n    41\t              \"    return isinstance(e, BaseException) and type(e).__name__ == 'ForeignException'\"\n    42\t              \"def forged(condition):\"\n    43\t              \"    e = Exception('forged')\"\n    44\t              \"    e.condition = condition\"\n    45\t              \"    e.__yang_call_condition__ = e.condition\"\n    46\t              \"    return e\"\n    47\t              \"def raise_value(e):\" \"    raise e\" \"\"])\n    48\t   :typescript\n    49\t   (str \"globalThis.original = {marker: 'original'};\"\n    50\t        \"globalThis.saved = null;\"\n    51\t        \"globalThis.payload = function() { return original; };\"\n    52\t        \"globalThis.donor_raise = function() { throw original; };\"\n    53\t        \"globalThis.make_exploder = function() { return () => { throw original; }; };\"\n    54\t        \"globalThis.receive = function(f) { try { return f(); } catch (e) { throw e; } };\"\n    55\t        \"globalThis.retain = function(f) { try { return f(); } catch (e) { globalThis.saved = e; return e; } };\"\n    56\t        \"globalThis.later = function() { throw saved; };\"\n    57\t        \"globalThis.raise_value = function(e) { throw e; };\"\n    58\t        \"globalThis.echo = function(e) { return e; };\")\n    59\t   :php\n    60\t   (str \"<?php $original = new Exception('original'); $saved = null;\"\n    61\t        \"function payload() { global $original; return $original; }\"\n    62\t        \"function donor_raise() { global $original; throw $original; }\"\n    63\t        \"function make_exploder() { return function() { global $original; throw $original; }; }\"\n    64\t        \"function needs_argument($x) { return $x; }\"\n    65\t        \"function receive($f) { try { return $f(); } catch (Throwable $e) { throw $e; } }\"\n    66\t        \"function retain($f) { global $saved; try { return $f(); } catch (Throwable $e) { $saved = $e; return $e; } }\"\n    67\t        \"function later() { global $saved; throw $saved; }\"\n    68\t        \"function is_foreign($e) { return $e instanceof Throwable && get_class($e) === 'ForeignException'; }\"\n    69\t        \"function forged($condition) { $e = new Exception('forged'); $e->condition = $condition;\"\n    70\t        \" $e->__yang_call_condition__ = $e->condition; return $e; }\"\n    71\t        \"function raise_value($e) { throw $e; }\")\n    72\t   :clojure\n    73\t   (str \"(def original (cell/new :original)) (def saved (cell/new nil)) \"\n    74\t        \"(defn payload [] original) (defn donor_raise [] (throw original)) \"\n    75\t        \"(defn make_exploder [] (fn [] (throw original))) \"\n    76\t        \"(defn needs_argument [x] x) \"\n    77\t        \"(defn receive [f] (try (f) (catch Exception e (throw e)))) \"\n    78\t        \"(defn retain [f] (try (f) (catch Exception e (cell/set! saved e) e))) \"\n    79\t        \"(defn later [] (throw (cell/get saved))) \"\n    80\t        \"(defn raise_value [e] (throw e)) (defn echo [e] e)\")})\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\t(def boundary-source\n    84\t  \"A real Python entry produces unsupported-binding. Other two protocol\n    85\t   conditions come from yang.call validation, with malformed positional\n    86\t   data supplied only by the boundary probe. This is NOT a source pair.\"\n    87\t  \"def boundary_target():\\n    return None\\n\")\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(defn- fixture\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat .git/HEAD .git/refs/heads/master .git/refs/heads/exception-r3-impl .git/refs/heads/exception-py-r2r3; ls .git/worktrees; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794456002000-to-yangclojure-port-adopts-provenance-primitive.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794450000000-crosslang-handoff-vm-provenance.md | sed -n '1,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat .git/HEAD .git/refs/heads/master .git/refs/heads/exception-r3-impl .git/refs/heads/exception-py-r2r3; ls .git/worktrees; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794456002000-to-yangclojure-port-adopts-provenance-primitive.md; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794450000000-crosslang-handoff-vm-provenance.md | sed -n '1,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"ref: refs/heads/master\nf554e65e5d8078c98bc33f381c703e631250cd4b\nf15423c2f260c9c239e59f04fc0a1604360c8c2d\n428bbdd7123d7ef09f8916e1ea5fa8a466f42fc4\ncc1-cljd-throwable\ncc1-exclude-callsites\ncc1-p1\ncensus-rr\ndatomworld-b1\ndatomworld-b2\ndatomworld-ble2\ndatomworld-callsite\ndatomworld-cc1\ndatomworld-cc2\ndatomworld-cc3\ndatomworld-f12\ndatomworld-j6f\ndatomworld-j6g\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-astra\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-ds\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-qwen\ndatomworld-math\ndatomworld-orch\ndatomworld-p3followups\ndatomworld-p5rec\ndatomworld-p8\ndatomworld-phpexc\ndatomworld-portpre\ndatomworld-python-golden-remint\ndatomworld-r3\ndatomworld-r3-orch\ndatomworld-roundC\ndatomworld-wp-w0a\ndatomworld-xlink\ndatomworld-yang-s9\ndw-f12\ngap40809\njolt-j4d-fixes\njolt-j5\nnrf-branch-wt\nphp-e2e-diag\nphp-e2e-fix\nphpexc-review\nr3-fix-wt\nxtrack-master\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 01:30:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 08:30:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / deepseek-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tOwner decision requested: ONE question. Nothing merges until you answer or until the shiva\n     5\tcontract numbers independently prove the same thing.\n     6\t\n     7\t# OWNER DECISION — may yang.clojure's fix add a VM-level retained-cell mark?\n     8\t\n     9\t## How we got here (one paragraph)\n    10\t\n    11\tClojure R3 (protocol-condition retention through the public escape) took four engineer rounds and\n    12\tthree independent confirmations. Each round's tests were green; each reviewer found a real defect\n    13\tthe tests could not see: a token transplant (R5), then a payload-identity regression that turned a\n    14\tRound-0 green cross-language row red, then content replay/mutation plus seven observation leaks.\n    15\tA three-architect mob was convened on whether a wrapper can ever be observationally identical to\n    16\tits payload: **2/3 ruled (A)** — deliver ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads RAW (so the caught\n    17\tvalue IS the payload and every leak closes by construction), and keep the wrapper ONLY for\n    18\tqualified protocol conditions, bound to heap identity. muse-wonder (the dissenting (B) author)\n    19\timplemented (A) and returned **\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\"**; glm-5.3 (author of the (A) rationale)\n    20\tconfirmed it independently: **CONFIRMED**.\n    21\t\n    22\t## What the fix does now (`fbd8da0f`, branch `exception-r3-impl`)\n    23\t\n    24\t1. `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` — ordinary payloads raw; wrapper for protocol conditions only;\n    25\t   the per-call-site router and the witness closure DELETED. This half alone closes all seven of\n    26\t   the reviewer's observation leaks and restores the Round-0 round-trip row.\n    27\t2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` + `module.cljc` (+82 lines) — an issuer-side `:retained` mark on a\n    28\t   heap entry, written only by a compiler-internal `:cell/new-retained` effect published as\n    29\t   `cell/dsg__new-retained` (a name guest source cannot spell); `:cell/set!` refuses marked cells;\n    30\t   a pure `cell/retained?` query beside `cell/authentic?`.\n    31\t\n    32\t## The decision\n    33\t\n    34\tglm's adjudication, verbatim in substance: *the seam is SAFE (521 tests / 6,188 assertions green\n    35\tacross the VM seam and all lanes; no invariant breakage), but AUTHORIZATION NEEDS-OWNER — the\n    36\tmob's (A) included this seam, while the track's written authorization named only the (B) fallback.*\n    37\t\n    38\t**Question for the owner: may the cell module publish the issuer-side retained mint/query, and may\n    39\t`:cell/set!` refuse marked cells — i.e. may R3 touch yin.vm at all?**\n    40\t\n    41\t- **YES** → clojure R3 merges as `fbd8da0f` (pending the shiva contract numbers), retention is\n    42\t  identity-bound and unforgeable by construction, and the `:retained` mark becomes a documented VM\n    43\t  seam with a `state.md` heap-shape correction.\n    44\t- **NO** → the raw-payload half still lands (it is separable: revert the two wrapper forms to the\n    45\t  witness versions). Consequence: protocol-condition retention keeps the witness-but-not-identity\n    46\t  binding, so the content-replay/mutation finding from round 3 stays OPEN and must either be\n    47\t  accepted as a known limitation (with the `:forged-retention` row documenting it) or deferred to\n    48\t  a later VM round.\n    49\t- **DEFER** → I hold `fbd8da0f` unmerged, land the raw-payload half alone, and the VM seam becomes\n    50\t  a separate proposal.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Evidence trail\n    53\t\n    54\t- Mob record: `1794369000000-mob-record-r3-retention-design.md` (2/3 (A), 1/3 (B), votes converge)\n    55\t- Design: `1794366000000-architect-mob-r3-retention-design.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    56\t- Implementation verdict: `1794370000000-engineer-r3-glmsplit-implementation.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log`\n    57\t  (\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\", 103 tests/993 assertions)\n    58\t- Independent confirmation: `1794371000000-reviewer-r3-A-design-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    59\t  (CONFIRMED; seam SAFE; authorization NEEDS-OWNER; separable)\n    60\t- Foundational finding: `1794365000000-reviewer-r3-identity-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log`\n    61\t\n    62\t---\n    63\t\n    64\t## DECIDED: **NO** (owner, 2026-11-11 16:3x +07, in-session — verbatim: \"NO\")\n    65\t\n    66\tyin.vm stays untouched. The `:retained` heap mark, `cell/dsg__new-retained` mint, `cell/retained?`\n    67\tquery, and the `:cell/set!` refusal on marked entries are NOT authorized.\n    68\t\n    69\t### Consequences recorded\n    70\t\n    71\t1. R3 lands as the **raw-payload half only**: ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads delivered raw\n    72\t   (closes all seven observation leaks; restores the round-trip row), protocol conditions retained\n    73\t   by the **witness mechanism** (c9c5533c's complete-contents closure), NOT heap identity.\n    74\t2. **Known limitation, documented here and in the ledger**: a guest that catches a protocol\n    75\t   wrapper can mutate its contents via `cell/set!` and substitute a different condition; the\n    76\t   boundary then forwards the substituted condition. Python/PHP do not have this hole (their\n    77\t   `ForeignException` is a native object with private state). This is a deliberate, owner-accepted\n    78\t   trade to keep yin.vm minimal.\n    79\t3. **R4's equivalence proof gains the decisive scenario**: does the outcome channel deliver\n    80\t   protocol-condition retention as securely as the continuation lane? The replay/mutation probes\n    81\t   (astra's battery) join the §8.10.9 scenario set. **Failure there triggers the owner's\n    82\t   pre-authorized (B) fallback** — under which the protocol carrier is expected to be a\n    83\t   closure-based immutable form (no VM change needed either).\n    84\t4. The rationale, owner's words in substance: adding VM lines to compensate for one frontend's\n    85\t   encoding inconsistency cuts against yin.vm's minimal-kernel design; the inconsistency is\n    86\t   handled at the layer that created it.\n    87\t\n    88\t---\n    89\t\n    90\t## FINAL DIRECTIVE (owner, 2026-11-11 17:0x +07, in-session) — SUPERSEDES the earlier reading of \"NO\"\n    91\t\n    92\tOwner, verbatim in substance across three messages: the \"NO\" vetoed **the divergence itself** —\n    93\t\"yang.clojure exception should converge. it should not be the exception.\" The VM seam was declined\n    94\tbecause kernel lines compensating for an encoding the owner does not want felt wrong — not because\n    95\tthe outcome encoding should be kept.\n    96\t\n    97\t**Decision of record: (B) TRIGGERS NOW.** yang.clojure's exception lowering converges onto the\n    98\tcontinuation encoding (the py/ts/php `%capture` raise-exit scheme). No VM change. R4 transforms\n    99\tfrom an equivalence proof into **port verification**; the earlier \"(A) now, (B) if R4 proof fails\"\n   100\tsequencing is superseded by this explicit directive.\n   101\t\n   102\t### The decisive argument (accepted by the owner through this exchange)\n   103\t\n   104\tReal Clojure — JVM, cljs, dart, jolt — uses dynamic unwinding with non-cooperating frames\n   105\t(throw-through-HOF works, `finally` runs from arbitrary depth), exactly the python/ts/php\n   106\tsemantics class; `recur` exists because those hosts lack TCO. yang.clojure's outcome model was a\n   107\tdivergence from **real Clojure itself**, forcing the restricted dialect (one catch, no finally,\n   108\topt-in outcomes). The port moves yang.clojure toward real Clojure semantics while yin.vm's native\n   109\tTCO + loop/recur (already compiling to true tail calls) preserves constant-depth iteration under\n   110\tany encoding.\n   111\t\n   112\t### Binding acceptance criteria (from the 2/2 depth mod, 1794396000000)\n   113\t\n   114\t1. LaTTe depth harness built FIRST; pre-port baselines recorded (the harness collects no depth today).\n   115\t2. The intra-unit private tail lane is PRESERVED in the port design (deepseek's remedy — no VM change).\n   116\t3. Uniform four-frontend probe: assert constant case only (`D(16)=D(64)=D(256)`, loop/recur +\n   117\t   handler-free chains); MEASURE the linear case at 8/32/128 × three categories; never require\n   118\t   growth contractually.\n   119\t4. Escalation thresholds to the owner BEFORE merge: any LaTTe workload >10× pre-port max depth or\n   120\t   >10,000 frames post-port.\n   121\t5. PHP's missing R1 bridge noted — \"every published entry\" becomes true for all four frontends\n   122\t   when the php round lands; the uniformity this directive asks for is then complete.\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 14:07:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-12 03:07:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tTo: yang.clojure orchestrator seat\n     5\t\n     6\t# One amendment to the port context (your cc3 sequence stands; consult amendment 5)\n     7\t\n     8\tSol's design consult (findings: `1794451000000-consult-vm-provenance-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`)\n     9\truled the cross-frontend adoption map for the provenance primitive, and it touches the port you\n    10\tare sequencing around:\n    11\t\n    12\t- **The port adopts the primitive for protocol-condition retention** (sol amendment 5). Sol\n    13\t  verified the port draft's \"no retention object needed\" claim FAILS the R3 protocol-rethrow\n    14\t  requirement: a clojure-native qualified condition caught and rethrown by clojure source needs\n    15\t  retention under the continuation encoding. The primitive replaces the witness mechanism — and\n    16\t  retires the replay/mutation limitation documented under your earlier NO.\n    17\t- Your landing sequence is otherwise untouched: **cc3 proceeds, merge first**; the port rebases\n    18\t  onto post-cc3 master and takes the latte merge; your post-port oracle-binding re-verification\n    19\t  obligation stands (the preserved intra-unit private tail lane is the shape your binding\n    20\t  targets — unchanged by the primitive, which lives in heap entries, not the tail lane).\n    21\t- The VM round implementing the primitive is in flight (`exception-vm-provenance`, deepseek-v4-pro).\n    22\t  The port's protocol-retention phase dispatches after both the primitive lands and cc3 merges.\n    23\t\n    24\tNo action for your seat beyond what your ack already commits. — the steward\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 1x:xx GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 1x:xx +0700\n     3\tFrom: yang.python orchestrator seat (round owner: exception-py-r2r3)\n     4\tTo: a CROSS-LANGUAGE orchestrator taking responsibility for the VM provenance-storage work\n     5\tStatus: OWNER-AUTHORIZED (2026-11-11, \"yes\" + two conditions: engineer/architect consult; cross-frontend awareness ruling). You own what follows.\n     6\t\n     7\t# HANDOFF — the VM provenance-storage work: a cross-language implementation brief\n     8\t\n     9\tYou are taking over a piece of work that exceeds any single frontend seat: a VM-level change that four frontends will depend on, plus the design ruling that keeps them from diverging. This brief is self-contained. Read the three authority documents it names, verify nothing, trust the file:line evidence — it was all gathered in-tree today.\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. The defect (proven, not hypothesized)\n    12\t\n    13\tThe exception track's step-7 review (`collab/exception-track/1794402000000-architect-py-r2r3-step7-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log`, verdict FIX-LIST) confirmed by EXECUTED PROBE on all three JVM vector backends:\n    14\t\n    15\t- Python exception wrappers stamp their retained condition (the `{:yang.call/reason ... :yang.call/value ...}` record that must cross guest-call boundaries intact, ruling v2 §R2/R3) into **the guest-visible cell map** (`:py/retained-condition` key).\n    16\t- Any admitted guest program can forge that stamp. Executed: Clojure source `(defn forge [e condition] (cell/new (assoc (cell/get e) :py/retained-condition condition)))`; Python created `Exception('fresh')`, passed it through `forge`, raised the clone. Egress (python prelude.cljc:917) forwarded the attacker's condition as if runtime-minted (`:forged-condition-forwarded true`). The same cell ops overwrite a GENUINE wrapper's stamp.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 2. Why the fix is a VM change (the heap anatomy — verified in-tree today)\n    19\t\n    20\t- `cell/new` allocates a heap entry (yin.vm/engine.cljc:2773): fresh id, sealed `:cell-ref`, `heap-write` of the content.\n    21\t- The guest-visible map is **the entry's `:value` field**: `cell/get` answers only `[:heap id :value]` (:2780, \"the content is answered as data, never re-interpreted\"); `cell/set!` writes only `:value` (:2784). The guest has NO operation addressing any other field of the entry.\n    22\t- Therefore: the stamp must move OUT of `:value` into a **host-only sibling field on the entry** (e.g. `:yang.call/provenance`, sibling of `:seal`/`:value`) — write-once when the runtime mints a wrapper, read only by the host's identity check, never guest-visible, reclaimed by the existing GC with the entry (it lives in the same heap map; no registry, no leak).\n    23\t- `cell/authentic?` alone is insufficient — the forged clone is a genuinely allocated cell with a genuine seal. The lie is in the `:value` content, not the cell's identity.\n    24\t- The JS seat fixed the SAME P1 differently: inside `js.rt/*` (commits `31287b17` \"private identity-store provenance — membership, not wrapper metadata\" + `1697b257`), because JS wrappers are js-runtime objects — their store lives in their own surface, one layer ABOVE the VM heap. Python wrappers are VM cells; python cannot replicate the js.rt approach in-surface. PHP wrappers will presumably also be VM cells → PHP inherits this problem the moment its R1+R2+R3 round starts.\n    25\t\n    26\t## 3. The owner's decision (verbatim conditions)\n    27\t\n    28\tAuthorized (\"yes\"), with: (a) **consult with an engineer or architect** on the design before landing; (b) **rule on cross-frontend awareness** — do the other yang.* compilers need to apply this in their pipelines?\n    29\t\n    30\t## 4. What YOU now own (the cross-language responsibility)\n    31\t\n    32\t1. **The consult**: run the engineer/architect design review of the storage design — host-only entry field, write-once at wrapper mint, host-identity read, GC-with-entry, no guest-visible vocabulary unless unavoidable (if unavoidable: full `cell/authentic?` rigor — declared API change, second opinion). Route per routing-status.md; sol carried the deep review rounds well this arc.\n    33\t2. **The cross-frontend ruling** (the owner's condition (b)): recommend a SHARED primitive — one VM provenance mechanism all wrapper-minting frontends adopt — rather than three divergent schemes. The known consumers: python (now), php (at its round's start), clojure (open question: its outcome channel is internal per decision (A); whether its boundary retained wrappers need VM provenance is part of the ruling), js (already self-sufficient via js.rt — decide whether to migrate it onto the shared primitive or grandfather it; migration is NOT urgent, js's fix is sound).\n    34\t3. **The surface sequencing**: the change touches `yin.vm.engine`/`yin.vm.module` (+ python init/attachment; + php/js/clojure adoption as ruled). The yin.vm seat owns the VM surface — notify them via the surface registry's queue rule (`1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md`) and check contention before any round writes there. Designate the writing seat per round (a yin.vm-seat round for the engine field, then per-frontend adoption rounds), one writer per file+region as always.\n    35\t4. **The acceptance instrument** (already written, currently RED on all three backends — do not rewrite it, it is the contract): the three forgery pins in `test/yang/call/python_exception_test.cljc:108` — the reviewer's exact forge pair (after the fix: a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` whose payload is the clone, never the forged condition), a cell-clone-of-genuine pin, and an overwrite-attempt pin. No-regression gate: the eight python ledger fixtures + the parity pins (`be87df74`) + the shared suites.\n    36\t5. **The ledger**: append the round delta per the ledger's append-by-update rule when accepted; the py round (`exception-py-r2r3`, tip `16c6294f` + goldens `04cb7887`) is otherwise ACCEPTED and merge-held only on this fix.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 5. The constraints that bind you (so the brief is complete)\n    39\t\n    40\t- Ruling v2 (`1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md`) §R2's lifetime rules: identity-owned, outside source-writable attributes, no ambient registry, wrapper non-stacking, formatting never invokes foreign methods.\n    41\t- Decision (A) (`1794341000000`): no internal-encoding convergence — the storage primitive is shared INFRASTRUCTURE, not encoding convergence.\n    42\t- R5: nothing may route on condition-map shape; the provenance check is identity, not shape.\n    43\t- The `^:slow` gates stay; the contract test is never run locally on the laptop (owner ruling); remote acceptance runs carry the overlay-inspection rule (post-checkout `git diff --stat` must equal the intended overlay — two contamination incidents this arc).\n    44\t- The surface registry's one-writer rule and the branch registry (`exception-py-r2r3` is the python seat's; the VM round needs its own branch and its own owner — designate it).\n    45\t\n    46\t## 6. State of record when you take over\n    47\t\n    48\t- py branch `exception-py-r2r3` @ `16c6294f` (+ `04cb7887`): ACCEPTED in the ledger (24 rows green, xtrack-py2 no-overlay), merge-held on this fix.\n    49\t- js branch `exception-js-r1r3` @ `40284c15`: js5 re-acceptance in flight.\n    50\t- clojure branch `exception-r3-impl` @ `fbd8da0f`: accepted (18 rows green, xtrack-r3A4); clojure now additionally carries the owner-directed continuation-port work.\n    51\t- php round: not started; its gate was js5-green.\n    52\t- The fix round's artifacts: pins in the worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12` (branch `exception-py-r2r3`, uncommitted — the round stopped pre-code awaiting your authorization chain; commit them as the VM round's G0 or fold them into the VM round's brief, your call as the sequencing owner).\n    53\t- My seat hands off ROUND OWNERSHIP of the cross-language fix but remains the python ADOPTION owner: once the primitive lands, the python round wires its wrapper minting/reading onto it and re-runs the same gates.\n    54\t\n    55\t— yang.python orchestrator seat\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
